    pr_ref:  # d4ab
    description: "The std::io share of WI-lutuh's 17 unenumerated rust modules.\n\nMODULES: std::io, std::io::BufReader.\n\nWHY THEY REFUSE. std::io is the stdio boundary itself -- stdout, stderr, stdin,\nand the Read / Write / Seek / BufRead traits every other reader and writer\ndelegates to. Note the VOCABULARY TRAP the python audit recorded and that\napplies here unchanged: the `logging` boundary covers stdout/stderr, so a module\nthat PRINTS does I/O. std::io prints.\n\nBufReader IS THE INTERESTING CASE AND IT NEARLY ESCAPED. It performs every read\nthrough a generic `R: Read`, so `std/src/io/buffered/bufreader.rs` contains no\nsyscall text at all -- the first version of the probe reported NO SIGNAL for a\ntype whose entire purpose is reading. It was caught by adding a delegation\nsignal (a type that implements or requires an I/O trait IS an I/O surface no\nmatter where the syscall physically lives), not by reading the source.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THE AUDIT: the same delegation applies to BufWriter, LineWriter,\nCursor, Take, Chain, Empty, Repeat, Sink, and to std::io::copy / read_to_string\n/ stdin().lock() and friends. Enumerating std::io means enumerating the trait\nsurface, not a function list, and deciding what a wrapper type inherits from its\nwrapped reader. That decision is the substance of this item.\n\nEVIDENCE BAR: total surface, EXACT matching, refuse-by-default. The python\naudit's unaided first draft scored 16% wrong, every error a false all-clear.\nInstrument: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/lutuh_rust_08262026/probe.py.\n\nPAY EXPECTATION: completing rust's stdlib audit changes no verdict on either\ntestable rust repo today -- the uncatalogued-module check is the LAST rung of\n`boundary_coverage`'s early-return ladder. See the note on WI-lutuh."  # d4ab
    fields: {}  # d4ab
- op: discuss  # 5558
  at: '2026-08-27T04:52:13Z'  # 5558
  by: agent  # 5558
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 5558
  clock: 2  # 5558
  nonce: '5558'  # 5558
  message: "DONE. std::io enumerated in total (556 items across 15 files of the installed\nrustc 1.94.0 library/std/src), 40 rows added, every refusal pinned as a test.\nPR: see pr_ref. The item's own question is answered, and the answer generalised\npast it.\n\nTHE ITEM ASKED: what does a wrapper (BufReader) inherit from the reader it\nwraps. THE ENUMERATION ANSWERED A STRUCTURAL QUESTION FIRST: where may a row be\nkeyed at all. A row's module must be a type RECEIVER INFERENCE CAN PRODUCE, and\nthree kinds cannot be:\n\n * A TRAIT. No rust value has type `Read` -- values have type File, TcpStream,\n   BufReader<R>. gate_named_entry drops method-kind hits when there is no\n   module hint, and a trait can never BE that hint. MEASURED: every edge whose\n   slot is std::io::Read / ::Write / ::BufRead in the 42-repo cohort carries\n   the name `module` -- a `use` IMPORT, never a call. So Read / Seek / BufRead\n   get NO ROWS. (The 4 pre-existing std::io::Write rows are KEPT, on the narrow\n   ground that a Box<dyn Write> receiver could in principle resolve to the\n   trait; they are inert otherwise. They have never starved their module only\n   because method_starved_modules counts `calls` edges and an import is not\n   one -- that is luck, and it is now pinned by a test.)\n\n * A WRAPPER -- THE ITEM'S OWN QUESTION, AND THE ANSWER IS MEASURED, NOT\n   REASONED. A wrapper's boundary is the boundary of what it wraps, which the\n   catalogue cannot know. In `rage`, the one cohort repo that uses BufReader\n   heavily, 8 of ~10 BufReader::new sites wrap an IN-MEMORY BYTE SLICE\n   (TEST_SK.as_bytes()), not a file. An fs_read row on BufReader would be\n   MAJORITY-WRONG on the first real repository that exercises it. NOTE THIS\n   LANDS OPPOSITE TO REFUSE-BY-DEFAULT INTUITION, which would have added it.\n   The boundary belongs at the wrapped thing's own constructor; File::open is\n   already rowed. Same verdict for BufWriter, LineWriter, Cursor, Take, Chain.\n\n * A GENERIC HELPER. io::copy's measured call site in rage is\n   `fn write_output<R: io::Read, W: io::Write>(mut input: R, mut output: W)`\n   -- generic BY CONSTRUCTION, so its boundary is unknowable rather than merely\n   unknown. Same for io::read_to_string. Both ABSENT.\n\nWHERE THE ROWS WENT INSTEAD: THE CONCRETE TYPE. Not a new invention -- it is\nwhat std::net::TcpStream ALREADY did (read/read_to_end/write/write_all, all\ntrait-derived names, on the concrete type) and what python.yaml does with\nWI-fuvuj's synthetic `file` module rather than putting them on io.IOBase.\nstd::fs::File was the inconsistency: it implements Read, Write and Seek and\ncarried NONE of their methods. Added File.{read,read_vectored,read_to_end,\nread_to_string,read_exact} fs_read and File.{write,write_vectored,write_all,\nwrite_fmt} fs_write, plus Stdin/StdinLock (ipc_recv) and Stdout/Stderr/\nStdoutLock/StderrLock (logging) -- the Lock variants because `.lock()` moves no\ndata, so without them a read through stdin().lock() is the same gap one level\ndown.\n\nREFUSALS, each with its reason and its test: seek/rewind/seek_relative/\nstream_position (a syscall, but nothing crosses -- the try_clone rule);\nflush (python.yaml excludes it deliberately: commits writes already counted,\nand a flush row there made every bare .flush() read as an unwrapped fs-write);\nby_ref/bytes/chain/take (adapters); empty/repeat/sink (null devices); pipe\n(descriptor plumbing, python precedent); .lock(); Error/ErrorKind (70 of the\n102 measured bare-std::io edges are error handling, and none of it is I/O);\neverything unstable in rustc 1.94.0.\n\nTHE ROWS ARE CORRECT AND INERT, AND I AM SAYING SO RATHER THAN LETTING IT BE\nFOUND LATER. Measured across 11,691 rust call edges in the 42-repo cohort, the\nrust catalogue matches ZERO method-kind primitives -- every match is\nfunction-kind. The 78 real trait-method call edges all arrive as\nrust:external:0-0:<name>:external_symbol with a null dst_ref. That is INV-linub\nL3/L4 and it makes EVERY method row in rust.yaml dead today, TcpStream's\nincluded. Both measurements were run: starvation delta across 42 surveys =\nnothing freed, nothing newly starved (so the method-only Stdin/StdinLock\nmodules introduced no new starvation); classification delta = 0 edges\nreclassified. The item's own PAY EXPECTATION (\"changes no verdict on either\ntestable rust repo today\") is CONFIRMED, for a reason one rung deeper than it\npredicted. Filed the rust arm of that on INV-linub rather than opening a\nduplicate.\n\nINSTRUMENTS, all production-backed (no reimplemented predicate or loader):\nenum_io.py (total-surface enumerator, brace-depth stack, designed against all\nthree WI-bupor probe bugs), measure_bupor.py (starvation A/B, old arm = HEAD's\nyaml in a temp catalog dir), measure_fire.py (classification A/B),\nwhy_inert.py (diagnosis + TcpStream positive control)."  # 5558
